:db/id "step-23b5296c-9050-57da-ab12-b54ca52a68dd" :step/id #uuid "23b5296c-9050-57da-ab12-b54ca52a68dd" :step/content 17592186259605 :step/next "step-de912477-5c57-5e4d-865f-02d49e044bb5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-de912477-5c57-5e4d-865f-02d49e044bb5" :step/id #uuid "de912477-5c57-5e4d-865f-02d49e044bb5" :step/content 17592186134086 :step/next "step-8338d321-9ae2-5f16-a804-d4198a730ec4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8338d321-9ae2-5f16-a804-d4198a730ec4" :step/id #uuid "8338d321-9ae2-5f16-a804-d4198a730ec4" :step/content 17592186387498 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096743 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d26b4fec-f259-5757-943d-c92ed32d5a4b" "question-f593ac8e-7a29-5f43-82ff-aea84a5ba69c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d26b4fec-f259-5757-943d-c92ed32d5a4b" :question/id #uuid "d26b4fec-f259-5757-943d-c92ed32d5a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-10859" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $10{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{10{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(10-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $\\frac{2}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d7987cd-9189-59ea-8be9-6b86123a7745"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d7987cd-9189-59ea-8be9-6b86123a7745" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7987cd-9189-59ea-8be9-6b86123a7745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78d710b0-36b9-5af3-afee-6ec469874bb2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a58b002-6268-59f9-8d62-d5780db00a57" "answer-975f8bf1-29e1-5e24-8470-e16a6bceb947" "answer-78d710b0-36b9-5af3-afee-6ec469874bb2" "answer-e2169256-da89-5b10-b1ae-084da0651fe3" "answer-1084ac63-ef9b-56cc-bcb8-ef50cf2a751e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a58b002-6268-59f9-8d62-d5780db00a57" :answer/id #uuid "1a58b002-6268-59f9-8d62-d5780db00a57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-975f8bf1-29e1-5e24-8470-e16a6bceb947" :answer/id #uuid "975f8bf1-29e1-5e24-8470-e16a6bceb947" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78d710b0-36b9-5af3-afee-6ec469874bb2" :answer/id #uuid "78d710b0-36b9-5af3-afee-6ec469874bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2169256-da89-5b10-b1ae-084da0651fe3" :answer/id #uuid "e2169256-da89-5b10-b1ae-084da0651fe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1084ac63-ef9b-56cc-bcb8-ef50cf2a751e" :answer/id #uuid "1084ac63-ef9b-56cc-bcb8-ef50cf2a751e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f593ac8e-7a29-5f43-82ff-aea84a5ba69c" :question/id #uuid "f593ac8e-7a29-5f43-82ff-aea84a5ba69c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123332" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}=\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-9{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{n}-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-0}{0-9} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0759db57-b8ce-5f50-b46e-8b442e52ee62"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0759db57-b8ce-5f50-b46e-8b442e52ee62" :answer-field/id #uuid "0759db57-b8ce-5f50-b46e-8b442e52ee62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ddcaa7ca-0c0c-5d03-adf8-e0d7f6832969" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ddcaa7ca-0c0c-5d03-adf8-e0d7f6832969" "answer-973d5ba1-9f1d-5d54-a5a6-681dae8dfc0a" "answer-ba2648e8-3c45-5b47-8bbc-61b0f969add5" "answer-5ca0fb74-d0d3-5f86-be12-a821fb96b41e" "answer-fa7ed841-cc61-56c4-b2ed-0e53c44c292c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddcaa7ca-0c0c-5d03-adf8-e0d7f6832969" :answer/id #uuid "ddcaa7ca-0c0c-5d03-adf8-e0d7f6832969" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-973d5ba1-9f1d-5d54-a5a6-681dae8dfc0a" :answer/id #uuid "973d5ba1-9f1d-5d54-a5a6-681dae8dfc0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba2648e8-3c45-5b47-8bbc-61b0f969add5" :answer/id #uuid "ba2648e8-3c45-5b47-8bbc-61b0f969add5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ca0fb74-d0d3-5f86-be12-a821fb96b41e" :answer/id #uuid "5ca0fb74-d0d3-5f86-be12-a821fb96b41e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa7ed841-cc61-56c4-b2ed-0e53c44c292c" :answer/id #uuid "fa7ed841-cc61-56c4-b2ed-0e53c44c292c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096741 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-548006ed-ff60-5fe8-9b17-af5147ac7aae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-548006ed-ff60-5fe8-9b17-af5147ac7aae" :question/id #uuid "548006ed-ff60-5fe8-9b17-af5147ac7aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-347972" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{12n+1}{3n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}{\\frac{3n}{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12+\\frac{1}{n}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12+0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c43acc61-3f4b-58f8-9442-6e3ef8f460f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c43acc61-3f4b-58f8-9442-6e3ef8f460f7" :answer-field/id #uuid "c43acc61-3f4b-58f8-9442-6e3ef8f460f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ea68bf8b-4deb-5b99-92c1-18ea456b7de6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea68bf8b-4deb-5b99-92c1-18ea456b7de6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea68bf8b-4deb-5b99-92c1-18ea456b7de6" :answer/id #uuid "ea68bf8b-4deb-5b99-92c1-18ea456b7de6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a4edf402-bea0-5a44-bc5e-8ea032b32554" :activity/id #uuid "a4edf402-bea0-5a44-bc5e-8ea032b32554" :activity/title "Limits of Sequences" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049983 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-06677fb3-7ea6-5165-8cd4-81f9a96fa023" "step-1718a7b4-2d06-538f-8e12-c06876a48a52" "step-662b9fe1-3bf2-5624-8370-6f85b744866a" "step-07528adf-f296-5070-8943-009a2dae445f" "step-55189c97-78f2-5c38-9d2b-e6e0a11aa6ba"] :activity/first-step "step-06677fb3-7ea6-5165-8cd4-81f9a96fa023" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-06677fb3-7ea6-5165-8cd4-81f9a96fa023" :step/id #uuid "06677fb3-7ea6-5165-8cd4-81f9a96fa023" :step/content "question-d26b4fec-f259-5757-943d-c92ed32d5a4b" :step/next "step-1718a7b4-2d06-538f-8e12-c06876a48a52" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1718a7b4-2d06-538f-8e12-c06876a48a52" :step/id #uuid "1718a7b4-2d06-538f-8e12-c06876a48a52" :step/content 17592186416142 :step/next "step-662b9fe1-3bf2-5624-8370-6f85b744866a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-662b9fe1-3bf2-5624-8370-6f85b744866a" :step/id #uuid "662b9fe1-3bf2-5624-8370-6f85b744866a" :step/content 17592186247229 :step/next "step-07528adf-f296-5070-8943-009a2dae445f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-07528adf-f296-5070-8943-009a2dae445f" :step/id #uuid "07528adf-f296-5070-8943-009a2dae445f" :step/content "question-f593ac8e-7a29-5f43-82ff-aea84a5ba69c" :step/next "step-55189c97-78f2-5c38-9d2b-e6e0a11aa6ba" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-55189c97-78f2-5c38-9d2b-e6e0a11aa6ba" :step/id #uuid "55189c97-78f2-5c38-9d2b-e6e0a11aa6ba" :step/content "question-548006ed-ff60-5fe8-9b17-af5147ac7aae" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186051656 :answer-field/choices 17592186051657] [:db/retract 17592186051656 :answer-field/choices 17592186051658] [:db/retract 17592186051656 :answer-field/choices 17592186051659] [:db/retract 17592186051656 :answer-field/choices 17592186051660] [:db/retract 17592186051656 :answer-field/choices 17592186051661] {:db/id 17592186054770 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-60d9e1c1-b611-5995-967c-6c66ed571c82"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-60d9e1c1-b611-5995-967c-6c66ed571c82" :question/id #uuid "60d9e1c1-b611-5995-967c-6c66ed571c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-149565" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-5z\\le -1.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-5.$ To isolate $z,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-5.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5z\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-1 \\\\ \\frac{-5z}{-5}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-1}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5z}{-5}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-5$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5z}{-5} & \\ge \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}z}{\\cancel{-5}} & \\ge \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge \\frac{1}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b60c1271-2aa2-5db6-a4fc-0ced692f3cad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b60c1271-2aa2-5db6-a4fc-0ced692f3cad" :answer-field/id #uuid "b60c1271-2aa2-5db6-a4fc-0ced692f3cad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-11e4be9b-77ce-5d2c-870a-9582c9f4eacc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b94c625a-806d-5398-a511-8434b5363807" "answer-91b033fa-0ab3-5c2d-87b8-9809d0825695" "answer-11e4be9b-77ce-5d2c-870a-9582c9f4eacc" "answer-729d4612-b4b3-52d6-a984-56bcadb47c63" "answer-9bb97e3f-8e17-5d53-9ebf-0d14a1a104c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b94c625a-806d-5398-a511-8434b5363807" :answer/id #uuid "b94c625a-806d-5398-a511-8434b5363807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\ge -\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91b033fa-0ab3-5c2d-87b8-9809d0825695" :answer/id #uuid "91b033fa-0ab3-5c2d-87b8-9809d0825695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\ge 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11e4be9b-77ce-5d2c-870a-9582c9f4eacc" :answer/id #uuid "11e4be9b-77ce-5d2c-870a-9582c9f4eacc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\ge \\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-729d4612-b4b3-52d6-a984-56bcadb47c63" :answer/id #uuid "729d4612-b4b3-52d6-a984-56bcadb47c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\le -\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bb97e3f-8e17-5d53-9ebf-0d14a1a104c9" :answer/id #uuid "9bb97e3f-8e17-5d53-9ebf-0d14a1a104c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\le \\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054772 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6005239-b6a4-5801-8173-53f2d74d92ea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6005239-b6a4-5801-8173-53f2d74d92ea" :question/id #uuid "a6005239-b6a4-5801-8173-53f2d74d92ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-153600" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4\\le x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "To isolate $x,$ we need to perform the *opposite* of *adding* $7,$ which is *subtracting* $7.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\le x+7 \\\\ 4-7 & \\le x+7-7 \\\\ -3 & \\le x+0 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e410432f-86a4-50ea-8294-40d4d0eeeb2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e410432f-86a4-50ea-8294-40d4d0eeeb2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "e410432f-86a4-50ea-8294-40d4d0eeeb2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6e12aa20-0d6e-5360-a6c8-7346274c8177" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e12aa20-0d6e-5360-a6c8-7346274c8177" "answer-6395e4d6-160c-524d-bea0-39d0f0cb0acb" "answer-360ebf0c-440b-5b80-aa5d-f220ea76cfe8" "answer-60e57476-d7c3-5931-afaa-b90f394b253b" "answer-a9e80f2e-a321-5d67-acf6-345376f7ea8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e12aa20-0d6e-5360-a6c8-7346274c8177" :answer/id #uuid "6e12aa20-0d6e-5360-a6c8-7346274c8177" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge -3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6395e4d6-160c-524d-bea0-39d0f0cb0acb" :answer/id #uuid "6395e4d6-160c-524d-bea0-39d0f0cb0acb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-360ebf0c-440b-5b80-aa5d-f220ea76cfe8" :answer/id #uuid "360ebf0c-440b-5b80-aa5d-f220ea76cfe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60e57476-d7c3-5931-afaa-b90f394b253b" :answer/id #uuid "60e57476-d7c3-5931-afaa-b90f394b253b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le -3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9e80f2e-a321-5d67-acf6-345376f7ea8f" :answer/id #uuid "a9e80f2e-a321-5d67-acf6-345376f7ea8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055021 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{x}{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $2.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,4 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2}) & \\ge -8 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}2)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{2}) & \\ge -8 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & \\ge -8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & \\ge -8 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\ge -8 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\ge -8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051656 :answer-field/correct "answer-ba590d86-ddb8-598a-a220-46efedf147bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba590d86-ddb8-598a-a220-46efedf147bc" "answer-55632b59-8ff0-5f33-bd84-759756febdc5" "answer-249f7541-1a8a-52ec-9e0c-a25f296e0698" "answer-e10696b4-01d9-5bd2-8740-a24555a84567" "answer-99888988-7d3e-5baa-ab4a-decbb3967954"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba590d86-ddb8-598a-a220-46efedf147bc" :answer/id #uuid "ba590d86-ddb8-598a-a220-46efedf147bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge -8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55632b59-8ff0-5f33-bd84-759756febdc5" :answer/id #uuid "55632b59-8ff0-5f33-bd84-759756febdc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-249f7541-1a8a-52ec-9e0c-a25f296e0698" :answer/id #uuid "249f7541-1a8a-52ec-9e0c-a25f296e0698" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e10696b4-01d9-5bd2-8740-a24555a84567" :answer/id #uuid "e10696b4-01d9-5bd2-8740-a24555a84567" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le -8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99888988-7d3e-5baa-ab4a-decbb3967954" :answer/id #uuid "99888988-7d3e-5baa-ab4a-decbb3967954" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le 8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a0296a38-e916-56a7-bb47-0737bade1abd" :activity/id #uuid "a0296a38-e916-56a7-bb47-0737bade1abd" :activity/title "Solving One-Step Linear Inequalities" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049986 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-c4402b05-776a-5423-af2f-f5f1a382df37" "step-c0507717-60f0-50ac-81aa-8044cb88a14c" "step-b48eede8-bb40-5e1b-981b-25334c7cacd0"] :activity/first-step "step-c4402b05-776a-5423-af2f-f5f1a382df37" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c4402b05-776a-5423-af2f-f5f1a382df37" :step/id #uuid "c4402b05-776a-5423-af2f-f5f1a382df37" :step/content "question-60d9e1c1-b611-5995-967c-6c66ed571c82" :step/next "step-c0507717-60f0-50ac-81aa-8044cb88a14c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c0507717-60f0-50ac-81aa-8044cb88a14c" :step/id #uuid "c0507717-60f0-50ac-81aa-8044cb88a14c" :step/content "question-a6005239-b6a4-5801-8173-53f2d74d92ea" :step/next "step-b48eede8-bb40-5e1b-981b-25334c7cacd0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b48eede8-bb40-5e1b-981b-25334c7cacd0" :step/id #uuid "b48eede8-bb40-5e1b-981b-25334c7cacd0" :step/content 17592186055021 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "activity-26d0a5d9-5b60-5aa6-b2c6-cedad250e8b6" :activity/id #uuid "26d0a5d9-5b60-5aa6-b2c6-cedad250e8b6" :activity/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050188 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-9254b448-f46a-5f6e-bac5-fc3b31083f4a" "step-5f59b70c-7621-5b4b-9951-a03dc596f197" "step-5d2db7f9-adcc-596b-a786-26e75633f558" "step-ff2f0d5d-9693-56c0-8a11-c7a144fd0c57" "step-76294a87-9654-50ec-a44d-a6393dfb7851"] :activity/first-step "step-9254b448-f46a-5f6e-bac5-fc3b31083f4a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9254b448-f46a-5f6e-bac5-fc3b31083f4a" :step/id #uuid "9254b448-f46a-5f6e-bac5-fc3b31083f4a" :step/content 17592186278414 :step/next "step-5f59b70c-7621-5b4b-9951-a03dc596f197" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5f59b70c-7621-5b4b-9951-a03dc596f197" :step/id #uuid "5f59b70c-7621-5b4b-9951-a03dc596f197" :step/content 17592186278416 :step/next "step-5d2db7f9-adcc-596b-a786-26e75633f558" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5d2db7f9-adcc-596b-a786-26e75633f558" :step/id #uuid "5d2db7f9-adcc-596b-a786-26e75633f558" :step/content 17592186278420 :step/next "step-ff2f0d5d-9693-56c0-8a11-c7a144fd0c57" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ff2f0d5d-9693-56c0-8a11-c7a144fd0c57" :step/id #uuid "ff2f0d5d-9693-56c0-8a11-c7a144fd0c57" :step/content 17592186421236 :step/next "step-76294a87-9654-50ec-a44d-a6393dfb7851" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-76294a87-9654-50ec-a44d-a6393dfb7851" :step/id #uuid "76294a87-9654-50ec-a44d-a6393dfb7851" :step/content 17592186281388 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172016 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8972fb0a-9f6e-50fc-86fb-a54e4588df90"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8972fb0a-9f6e-50fc-86fb-a54e4588df90" :question/id #uuid "8972fb0a-9f6e-50fc-86fb-a54e4588df90" :question/math-academy-id "q-50133" :question/problem "![](images/93/9389d3d0d9206b72f379a328819e4624da3a294fc3222f048edc53957a8679ac.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](images/31/310d05d05284201c4914de171e6ee6a7574784da4c9bf7d4e442cdf2a210722d.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same vertical line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $y.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={e}^{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $x\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{1}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb57608e-2ee3-5d75-9465-c30eb8072dad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb57608e-2ee3-5d75-9465-c30eb8072dad" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb57608e-2ee3-5d75-9465-c30eb8072dad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aaff3195-01f3-5afb-88f1-a42822d0cbe3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aaff3195-01f3-5afb-88f1-a42822d0cbe3" "answer-4a90b7e9-8188-53a6-95fc-db777d59155f" "answer-007e39a0-05eb-578a-a48d-5694fcd3c657" "answer-6324d0c2-5334-554d-8b13-506fc5b5dbb6" "answer-9f9da200-0ade-54bf-b021-af17d6df52d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaff3195-01f3-5afb-88f1-a42822d0cbe3" :answer/id #uuid "aaff3195-01f3-5afb-88f1-a42822d0cbe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a90b7e9-8188-53a6-95fc-db777d59155f" :answer/id #uuid "4a90b7e9-8188-53a6-95fc-db777d59155f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-007e39a0-05eb-578a-a48d-5694fcd3c657" :answer/id #uuid "007e39a0-05eb-578a-a48d-5694fcd3c657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6324d0c2-5334-554d-8b13-506fc5b5dbb6" :answer/id #uuid "6324d0c2-5334-554d-8b13-506fc5b5dbb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f9da200-0ade-54bf-b021-af17d6df52d1" :answer/id #uuid "9f9da200-0ade-54bf-b021-af17d6df52d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172018 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e7d6da00-83dc-5f7a-b7e3-c9a339c8d8c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7d6da00-83dc-5f7a-b7e3-c9a339c8d8c8" :question/id #uuid "e7d6da00-83dc-5f7a-b7e3-c9a339c8d8c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-50157" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b45ab87c3b5f6b083ca5828676ca96486e3825b3b3fbbfc3f31d19fa65a215b5.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ef/ef837c5bdd4198651302e77b70d592c7771071a1ee3543ce76a3685b0439fa82.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=-2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x+1)(y+2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+1)^{2}(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $y=-2$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-476162ce-d7d2-51ce-9444-b2f25f2ae279"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-476162ce-d7d2-51ce-9444-b2f25f2ae279" :answer-field/id #uuid "476162ce-d7d2-51ce-9444-b2f25f2ae279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c2b99d07-755c-5bef-bc57-98238ed75afa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2b99d07-755c-5bef-bc57-98238ed75afa" "answer-ffca65b3-4207-5784-b911-da4d96590ea7" "answer-eaf33ee7-68ac-5522-9117-9e92aa0afd2e" "answer-0026c51e-4d80-5bc2-8461-49af596e4e5d" "answer-3c7b084c-10ab-51da-a2b3-418e19f04fb7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2b99d07-755c-5bef-bc57-98238ed75afa" :answer/id #uuid "c2b99d07-755c-5bef-bc57-98238ed75afa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffca65b3-4207-5784-b911-da4d96590ea7" :answer/id #uuid "ffca65b3-4207-5784-b911-da4d96590ea7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eaf33ee7-68ac-5522-9117-9e92aa0afd2e" :answer/id #uuid "eaf33ee7-68ac-5522-9117-9e92aa0afd2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(y+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0026c51e-4d80-5bc2-8461-49af596e4e5d" :answer/id #uuid "0026c51e-4d80-5bc2-8461-49af596e4e5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x-1)(y-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c7b084c-10ab-51da-a2b3-418e19f04fb7" :answer/id #uuid "3c7b084c-10ab-51da-a2b3-418e19f04fb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172017 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cd64c565-f297-5346-b626-26857f890c09" "question-a2ee811e-ffbe-53ee-9b06-888771cc6f39"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172601 :question/problem "![](images/32/32165a622d57d6af278b7ce288093ef4db6867619046b8625071f0274a1c7e93.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}$\n\nII. ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n\nIII. ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a2/a278676af009b7096bd50f9b3918f0e8090c1822a9e029ac2b4b6325d1ba7a4c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}={x}^{3}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171231 :answer-field/correct 17592186171236 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd64c565-f297-5346-b626-26857f890c09" :question/id #uuid "cd64c565-f297-5346-b626-26857f890c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-80709" :question/problem "![](images/84/84597fa3c8e0a187df5792e8a8289033e6549599b1df5a34073049a5b3322770.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/36/3673d384035d7febd8540e34143c2a490f7aa3968752785ef809cff16d2524ce.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2x=x(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}={x}^{2}(2-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x<2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x=x(x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a19fb674-3a6d-5382-99ac-6066604aff59"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a19fb674-3a6d-5382-99ac-6066604aff59" :answer-field/id #uuid "a19fb674-3a6d-5382-99ac-6066604aff59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2542a06d-9303-52fb-97e7-add9f98d7b2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a697427-3416-5d0c-94de-b62ae70838f4" "answer-2eee5914-1ac4-5e4e-bd6b-cdcfec586af0" "answer-12ba6730-d2d1-5ea5-80ef-8e8c38878511" "answer-2542a06d-9303-52fb-97e7-add9f98d7b2c" "answer-bc223e25-78a4-519a-96e6-70f92377c549"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a697427-3416-5d0c-94de-b62ae70838f4" :answer/id #uuid "9a697427-3416-5d0c-94de-b62ae70838f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2x+2xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eee5914-1ac4-5e4e-bd6b-cdcfec586af0" :answer/id #uuid "2eee5914-1ac4-5e4e-bd6b-cdcfec586af0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12ba6730-d2d1-5ea5-80ef-8e8c38878511" :answer/id #uuid "12ba6730-d2d1-5ea5-80ef-8e8c38878511" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2542a06d-9303-52fb-97e7-add9f98d7b2c" :answer/id #uuid "2542a06d-9303-52fb-97e7-add9f98d7b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc223e25-78a4-519a-96e6-70f92377c549" :answer/id #uuid "bc223e25-78a4-519a-96e6-70f92377c549" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2ee811e-ffbe-53ee-9b06-888771cc6f39" :question/id #uuid "a2ee811e-ffbe-53ee-9b06-888771cc6f39" :question/math-academy-id "q-86442" :question/problem "![](images/90/9000c061a8826c4492400671b3ee8e6700420e1e553ceda47e6f086a474211c2.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a7/a71217d2b2e610b38f78f4471cc122de7098708900e3658cda449118df0e63dd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-41f0f8e8-48f1-5e24-ab08-3b3bcbba4475"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-41f0f8e8-48f1-5e24-ab08-3b3bcbba4475" :answer-field/id #uuid "41f0f8e8-48f1-5e24-ab08-3b3bcbba4475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4e6c4c42-b7c6-5745-94ba-7c3cfe7a7411" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d38d8b71-d94d-5a16-84ce-3da13f0fbb57" "answer-4302042c-8055-53af-bd3a-be8fd087c821" "answer-71203ad7-761f-586b-91f4-ea2287a2cb0f" "answer-8a594017-dd00-5e85-8323-b197a7f40e84" "answer-4e6c4c42-b7c6-5745-94ba-7c3cfe7a7411"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d38d8b71-d94d-5a16-84ce-3da13f0fbb57" :answer/id #uuid "d38d8b71-d94d-5a16-84ce-3da13f0fbb57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4302042c-8055-53af-bd3a-be8fd087c821" :answer/id #uuid "4302042c-8055-53af-bd3a-be8fd087c821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(y+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71203ad7-761f-586b-91f4-ea2287a2cb0f" :answer/id #uuid "71203ad7-761f-586b-91f4-ea2287a2cb0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x+x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a594017-dd00-5e85-8323-b197a7f40e84" :answer/id #uuid "8a594017-dd00-5e85-8323-b197a7f40e84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e6c4c42-b7c6-5745-94ba-7c3cfe7a7411" :answer/id #uuid "4e6c4c42-b7c6-5745-94ba-7c3cfe7a7411" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-430421d0-b0f8-5943-9c2b-aea2664e5f42" :activity/id #uuid "430421d0-b0f8-5943-9c2b-aea2664e5f42" :activity/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048885 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-44f60c0c-5d2f-59e4-aaaa-1e285c4c8203" "step-4063bcf2-9c80-51dd-b5c4-c46157272fde" "step-cffe8872-3258-5dea-971d-7ad093112cb1" "step-df0c9231-2d00-5a28-90a3-a4c777823c60" "step-082ae2ae-0aa5-5392-9f40-e5a1a5b40f7e"] :activity/first-step "step-44f60c0c-5d2f-59e4-aaaa-1e285c4c8203" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-44f60c0c-5d2f-59e4-aaaa-1e285c4c8203" :step/id #uuid "44f60c0c-5d2f-59e4-aaaa-1e285c4c8203" :step/content "question-8972fb0a-9f6e-50fc-86fb-a54e4588df90" :step/next "step-4063bcf2-9c80-51dd-b5c4-c46157272fde" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4063bcf2-9c80-51dd-b5c4-c46157272fde" :step/id #uuid "4063bcf2-9c80-51dd-b5c4-c46157272fde" :step/content "question-e7d6da00-83dc-5f7a-b7e3-c9a339c8d8c8" :step/next "step-cffe8872-3258-5dea-971d-7ad093112cb1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cffe8872-3258-5dea-971d-7ad093112cb1" :step/id #uuid "cffe8872-3258-5dea-971d-7ad093112cb1" :step/content 17592186172601 :step/next "step-df0c9231-2d00-5a28-90a3-a4c777823c60" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-df0c9231-2d00-5a28-90a3-a4c777823c60" :step/id #uuid "df0c9231-2d00-5a28-90a3-a4c777823c60" :step/content "question-cd64c565-f297-5346-b626-26857f890c09" :step/next "step-082ae2ae-0aa5-5392-9f40-e5a1a5b40f7e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-082ae2ae-0aa5-5392-9f40-e5a1a5b40f7e" :step/id #uuid "082ae2ae-0aa5-5392-9f40-e5a1a5b40f7e" :step/content "question-a2ee811e-ffbe-53ee-9b06-888771cc6f39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073781 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9094ec48-ee4f-5d76-adf5-7427a959082d" "question-abf3e34b-133a-5d9e-997c-b480aa675d59" "question-15f86877-8327-59b3-8e4f-e39de73201de"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073650 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Rotating the point $P$ by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin gives the point $A,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/53/53128ffb3404a6fb27b096896f744b57e143ec2c5d630ae7ef9d80c256bfb43c.png)\n\n- Reflecting the point $A$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $D,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/23/234d8273aea8cfc59810b44f575887de0effe09d7ab95657b4d8b76baf822a04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $D.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073904 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-abee8b7a-4a45-595d-8f90-0e0bbc3678c1" "question-5d698685-38f5-5e4a-b709-e155fb9c6b96"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073590 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A shift of $2$ units down can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x,y-2).\n$$\n\n- A reflection in the $y$-axis can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y).\n$$\n\n- A shift of $1$ unit to the right can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x+1,y).\n$$\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $Q$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the shift. The image of the point $\\left(4,3\\right)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(4,3) \\mapsto  (4,3-2)=(4, 1) .\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we apply the reflection. The image of the point $\\left(4,1\\right)$ under the reflection is\n\n$$\n(4, 1) \\mapsto(-4,1) .\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the second shift. The image of the point $\\left(-4,1\\right)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(-4,1) \\mapsto (-4+1,1)=(-3, 1) .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-3,1).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073779 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ba81a168-e077-501b-a6b1-f0f2d8fe936c" "question-d9d0cd21-4afc-561e-80fc-e28f273aa2e6" "question-87e86a6c-a43c-5891-bdf0-9aeef9e7dff1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073905 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-76343f99-bb5f-5787-863a-2557d071c09b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073591 :question/problem "The point $P(5,14)$ is reflected across the line $y=x$ and then stretched according to the function $(x,y)  \\mapsto (x,ky).$ If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(14,25),$ then what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA reflection across the line $y=x$ can be represented by\n\n$$\n(x,y ) \\mapsto (y, x) .\n$$\n\n \nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(5,14 ) \\mapsto (14,5) .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, applying the stretch to $(14,5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(14, 5) \\mapsto (14,  5k) .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(14,25),$ by equating the $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k=25\\,⟹\\,k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9094ec48-ee4f-5d76-adf5-7427a959082d" :question/id #uuid "9094ec48-ee4f-5d76-adf5-7427a959082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-45198" :question/problem "![](images/77/776278c72c3569eb4ee7bb48c8c544d4731be0378c942256699f5350a15d4b53.png)\n\nThe point $P$ shown above is reflected across the $x$-axis and then translated $3$ units to the left.  Which of the given points is the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations?" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Reflecting the point $P$ across the $x$-axis gives the point $F,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/78/782b4ca00a0c7e091873aaafbcc439b9f63b209bd878da3393c81848f2c8f375.png)\n\n- Shifting the point $F$ to the left by $3$ units gives the point $C,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/f4/f40099c0f59f3e07fbe505a102a7b7f513dcb8bc36e5abb1382ee8e7b003b9f9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $C.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-05a6f705-a8ca-5027-8e1e-7ed16ea6ea53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-05a6f705-a8ca-5027-8e1e-7ed16ea6ea53" :answer-field/id #uuid "05a6f705-a8ca-5027-8e1e-7ed16ea6ea53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c07101b6-519b-5a88-8551-6533b9ad2d56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a891e785-ad3f-5780-a789-64e4bb808822" "answer-a9741633-dd63-54e4-85b5-212a2fef0ea6" "answer-c07101b6-519b-5a88-8551-6533b9ad2d56" "answer-ca607ac2-38c8-5add-80ca-99f8ed882fc5" "answer-6aadaa4c-8a18-5f9e-b277-fc20046c319b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a891e785-ad3f-5780-a789-64e4bb808822" :answer/id #uuid "a891e785-ad3f-5780-a789-64e4bb808822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9741633-dd63-54e4-85b5-212a2fef0ea6" :answer/id #uuid "a9741633-dd63-54e4-85b5-212a2fef0ea6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c07101b6-519b-5a88-8551-6533b9ad2d56" :answer/id #uuid "c07101b6-519b-5a88-8551-6533b9ad2d56" :answer/type :answer.type/ma